window.docContents[169].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template--Acceptance":{"contents":"* D12 — Acceptance as a gate. Acceptance is a gate against the specification the model\n  document proves — run, as proof-by-execution, rather than asserted. This is the\n  load-bearing link between the two documents: without it, everything the model proved\n  stops at the model's own boundary.* D13 — A paired A/B in the executable. Performance claims are a paired A/B in the\n  deployed executable. A microbenchmark ranks; it does not predict. An isolated win of a\n  factor of two and a half can be zero in the binary that ships, and the only measurement\n  that settles the question is the one taken in the executable whose behavior is being\n  claimed.* D14 — What each green signal covered. The rubric that costs the least and is skipped\n  the most: each green signal must name which of the changed lines it actually compiled or\n  ran. A pipeline that passes without compiling the changed path is evidence about the\n  paths it did compile and about nothing else, and quoting it as though it covered the\n  change is how two defects survive three green signals.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Deploying a domain model: the deployment template","header":"35.6. Acceptance","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template--Acceptance"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-five-steps--Step-5-___-Declare-the-boundary___-what-enters___-what-leaves___-and-what-it-must-satisfy":{"contents":"Last, say where the model's numbers come from, where they go, and what the model claims\nabout them. The first four steps fix what each quantity is; this one fixes what the\nmodule is. Its interface is a declared boundary of kind-typed\nports — the inputs it ingests, the outputs it emits, its parameters, and the conditional\noutputs with their deciders — and its claim is a relation to its\nmeasurement model: that the output equals, inverts, refines, or is bounded by the\nmeasurement the module stands for, carried as a named witness theorem with its hypotheses\nand a license per carrier. The boundary is also what makes Step 4's erasure deployable:\nthe distribution license says that only an extensive\noutput commutes with a re-carving of its input, so sharding and batching — the deployment\nspectrum below — rest on the boundary's license clause. With the boundary in place\nthe module states each requirement it makes against it: what its quantities must\nsatisfy, and by what evidence, a theorem or an observation\n(the requirement clause).MiniWriteOnce stops at Step 4; the step is shown by the tutorial's oscillator exhibit\nTutorial.HarmonicOscillatorRequirements. There\ncomplianceBoundary takes three stiffnesses in and puts one compliance out,\ncomplianceSpec is the specification boundary that bounds it — one member, the\nwall spring's own compliance — and the requirement hp1 names the boundedBy edge between\nthe two, with its theorem as witness and a Bool spot check evaluated at Float.\n#kind_contract_decide checks each boundary in the kernel and leaves the theorem behind;\n#kind_boundary_audit lists every site where a number enters or leaves with its\nevidence tier, and a site with none is a printed violation, not a review comment.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009The five steps","header":"18.1.5. Step 5 — Declare the boundary: what enters, what leaves, and what it must satisfy","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-five-steps--Step-5-___-Declare-the-boundary___-what-enters___-what-leaves___-and-what-it-must-satisfy"}});